# Identifier correspondence for HighContrastHomogenization

This catalog records the bijection used to shorten 1,015 imported identifiers whose declarations
or uses exceed the 100-character line cap at ordinary indentation. Of these, 691 original leaf
names already exceed 100 characters; the remaining 324 cannot fit their qualified proof contexts. Each replacement is applied
consistently to Lean code,
including declarations and references. It changes names while preserving the mathematical
statements and proof terms modulo those names. Every row links to the actual declaration
in the pinned Apache-2.0 upstream source; repeated declarations have separate source links.

The independent HighContrastHomogenization source is pinned to
[7a13dbcd8d6609264a713373f5c69ceeac870472](https://github.com/scottnarmstrong/HighContrastHomogenization/tree/7a13dbcd8d6609264a713373f5c69ceeac870472).
Its required CoarseGraining support is pinned to
[c7ddd76c08ade64fed1b8d2ca51be14dfee8deb4](https://github.com/scottnarmstrong/CoarseGraining/tree/c7ddd76c08ade64fed1b8d2ca51be14dfee8deb4)
and isolated in `HCPolySupport`. The five frozen headline endpoints keep their original names
and statements: `polynomial_entry`, `algebraic_convergence`, `uniform_homogenization`,
`polynomial_homogenization`, and `quenched_convergence` in namespace `HCPoly`.

Separate compiler-confirmed compatibility repairs remove 24 redundant `DecidableEq`
assumptions: four initial public lemmas and 20 additional public or private supporting lemmas.
Two independent-product lemmas replace `Fintype` with `Finite` and construct a `Fintype`
inside their proofs. Every other binder and conclusion is retained. The additional 22 repairs
were checked by applying each new proof under its complete original compiled theorem type,
including the original binder order and explicitness, through Lean's ordinary kernel.
The four initial repairs were checked under their exact pinned original headers and proofs.
The compatibility table below records every affected declaration separately.

Two segment-integral inequalities explicitly retain their original automatically
included `NeZero` instance, in the same binder order. The carrier truncation equality retains the
same Classical decision term with its scope localized. These scope changes were checked against
scratch re-elaborations of the full original proofs and their elaborated binder order. Three
elliptic-truncation bridge statements also retain the original Classical decision terms using
declaration-local scope; their complete pinned original proofs, theorem types, binder order
and explicitness were verified against the imported declarations.

The names below are leaf identifiers; namespace qualification is retained independently.
Sixteen existing method declarations use their existing namespace in a surrounding block,
retaining their fully qualified names. Thirty-two proof or definition body references use
explicit local namespace openings to fit the line cap, with public statement headers retained.

